WEBVTT

00:00:02.000 --> 00:00:05.145
Picture a table of ten at a busy restaurant.

00:00:05.145 --> 00:00:08.289
The kitchen could finish your whole order before touching anyone else's.

00:00:08.289 --> 00:00:11.434
Burger, fries, a drink, then on to the next diner.

00:00:11.434 --> 00:00:14.579
Correct, but painfully slow.

00:00:14.579 --> 00:00:17.723
No kitchen works that way.

00:00:17.723 --> 00:00:20.868
It interleaves.

00:00:20.868 --> 00:00:24.012
Your burger fires while your friend's fries cook and the drinks pour, ten orders moving at once.

00:00:24.012 --> 00:00:27.157
Much faster, on one condition.

00:00:27.157 --> 00:00:30.302
Your burger reaches you, not your friend.

00:00:30.302 --> 00:00:33.446
A database faces the very same choice with concurrent transactions.

00:00:33.446 --> 00:00:36.591
The two simplest options are the extremes.

00:00:36.591 --> 00:00:39.736
Run one at a time, or let everything run free.

00:00:39.736 --> 00:00:42.880
Both fail.

00:00:42.880 --> 00:00:46.025
Let us see how, and what the fix looks like.

00:00:46.025 --> 00:00:49.209
Start with Naive way 1.

00:00:49.209 --> 00:00:52.392
Run each transaction start to finish, then the next.

00:00:52.392 --> 00:00:55.576
Nothing interleaves, so nothing corrupts.

00:00:55.576 --> 00:00:58.760
But it is slow.

00:00:58.760 --> 00:01:01.943
A transaction spends most of its life waiting on a single IO.

00:01:01.943 --> 00:01:05.127
Picture a short stretch of time.

00:01:05.127 --> 00:01:08.311
At reed start, the processor stalls.

00:01:08.311 --> 00:01:11.494
At reed end, the IO is done.

00:01:11.494 --> 00:01:14.678
The gap between them is the IO lag, and through all of it the processor sits idle, doing nothing.

00:01:14.678 --> 00:01:18.912
Now scale that to a queue on a single server.

00:01:18.912 --> 00:01:23.146
T1 runs, then waits on its IO.

00:01:23.146 --> 00:01:27.379
Next, T2 hits a long IO, and because no one else may run in that gap, the server just sits there.

00:01:27.379 --> 00:01:31.613
T3 and T4 are next in line.

00:01:31.613 --> 00:01:35.847
They stay blocked behind T2's wait, far longer than their own work needs.

00:01:35.847 --> 00:01:40.081
Every idle gap is pure waste, and one slow transaction stalls the whole line.

00:01:40.081 --> 00:01:43.986
Now Naive way 2.

00:01:43.986 --> 00:01:47.892
Drop all coordination and let everything run.

00:01:47.892 --> 00:01:51.797
On different rows, fine.

00:01:51.797 --> 00:01:55.703
On the same row, three ways to corrupt it, and no error is ever raised.

00:01:55.703 --> 00:01:59.608
First, a lost update.

00:01:59.608 --> 00:02:03.513
Two fans play the same song at once, so the play count should climb by two, from one hundred to one hundred and two.

00:02:03.513 --> 00:02:07.419
But both transactions read the same one hundred, and both write one hundred and one.

00:02:07.419 --> 00:02:11.324
The second write lands right on top of the first.

00:02:11.324 --> 00:02:15.229
The play count ends at one hundred and one, not one hundred and two.

00:02:15.229 --> 00:02:19.135
One play silently vanishes.

00:02:19.135 --> 00:02:23.040
Second, a dirty read.

00:02:23.040 --> 00:02:26.946
One transaction reads another's uncommitted write, then the writer aborts, so the reader trusted a value that never existed.

00:02:26.946 --> 00:02:30.851
Third, a stale read.

00:02:30.851 --> 00:02:34.756
One transaction, on its own, should see a stable world.

00:02:34.756 --> 00:02:38.662
Read the same row twice and you should get the same value both times.

00:02:38.662 --> 00:02:42.567
That is what isolation promises.

00:02:42.567 --> 00:02:46.472
But here a committed write slips in between the two reads.

00:02:46.472 --> 00:02:50.378
The first read sees twenty, the second sees thirty.

00:02:50.378 --> 00:02:54.283
The transaction now disagrees with itself, on a row it only read.

00:02:54.283 --> 00:02:58.189
That is the read breaking down.

00:02:58.189 --> 00:03:02.094
Every transaction reports success, and the database holds a number no one-at-a-time run could produce.

00:03:02.094 --> 00:03:05.329
So neither extreme is usable.

00:03:05.329 --> 00:03:08.565
Put them on one axis, safety on the left, performance on the right.

00:03:08.565 --> 00:03:11.800
Serial, Naive 1, sits at the safe end.

00:03:11.800 --> 00:03:15.035
Always correct, no throughput.

00:03:15.035 --> 00:03:18.271
No coordination, Naive 2, sits at the fast end.

00:03:18.271 --> 00:03:21.506
Maximum speed, corrupted data.

00:03:21.506 --> 00:03:24.741
The fix is concurrency control, the sweet spot in the middle.

00:03:24.741 --> 00:03:27.977
Interleave only the swaps whose order does not matter.

00:03:27.977 --> 00:03:31.212
Serial-correct, at concurrent speed.

00:03:31.212 --> 00:03:34.954
So both naive ways are dead ends.

00:03:34.954 --> 00:03:38.695
Serial is correct but wastes the machine.

00:03:38.695 --> 00:03:42.437
Free-for-all is fast but corrupts the data.

00:03:42.437 --> 00:03:46.179
Every real database lives in the middle, interleaving only what is safe.

00:03:46.179 --> 00:03:49.921
That leaves two questions.

00:03:49.921 --> 00:03:53.662
What makes an interleaving correct, and how does an engine deliver it without serializing everything.

00:03:53.662 --> 00:03:57.404
The next page builds the tool the answer rests on, a graph you read for cycles.
